---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import NewsletterSignup from '../components/NewsletterSignup.astro';
import MyVideoAskSection from '../components/MyVideoAskSection.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Why YAIBOS: business context, useful skills, and control of your files"
  description="How models, context, skills, and tools affect AI business results. Why YAIBOS keeps your files portable when you change models or harnesses.">
  <Header activePage="why" />

  <main class="py-16 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 bg-white">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
      
      <!-- Top Kicker & Title -->
      <div class="space-y-4 pb-12 mb-12 border-b border-slate-200/80">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full border border-blue-200 bg-blue-50 text-xs font-mono text-blue-700 font-semibold shadow-xs">
          <span>Control of your AI setup</span>
        </div>

        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 font-sans leading-tight">
          Why keep your business files portable?
        </h1>

        <p class="text-lg sm:text-xl text-slate-600 font-light leading-relaxed">
          YAIBOS keeps company knowledge and procedures in files you control. This makes it easier to change AI tools without rebuilding your business context.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 1: The Equation -->
      <section class="space-y-8 mb-16">
        <div>
          <span class="text-xs font-mono text-blue-600 font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider block mb-1">
            A way to think about useful AI
          </span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 font-sans">
            Models, context, skills, and tools
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed font-sans text-base sm:text-lg">
            A capable model needs company context, reusable skills, and tools to get useful work done. I think of those factors as a multiplication:
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Formula Card -->
        <div class="elegant-card rounded-2xl p-8 sm:p-10 border border-blue-200 bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50/50 via-white to-slate-50 text-center shadow-xs">
          <span class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-500 uppercase tracking-widest block mb-3 font-semibold">
            Business AI utility
          </span>
          <div class="font-mono text-2xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold text-slate-900 tracking-tight flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-2 sm:gap-3">
            <span class="text-blue-600">U</span>
            <span class="text-slate-400 font-normal">=</span>
            <span class="text-slate-800">M</span>
            <span class="text-slate-400 font-normal">×</span>
            <span class="text-slate-800">C</span>
            <span class="text-slate-400 font-normal">×</span>
            <span class="text-amber-700">Skills</span>
            <span class="text-slate-400 font-normal">×</span>
            <span class="text-emerald-700">Tools</span>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-4 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-500 font-mono">
            Utility = Model intelligence × Context quality × Skills × Action tools
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Breakdown of the 4 Terms -->
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6 pt-4">
          
          <!-- Term 1: Model -->
          <div class="p-6 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-xs space-y-2">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
              <span class="font-mono font-bold text-lg text-blue-600">M: Model intelligence</span>
              <span class="text-[10px] font-mono px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-slate-100 text-slate-600">Reasoning</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed font-sans">
              The reasoning ability of the model, such as Claude, GPT, DeepSeek, or a local open-weights model.
            </p>
            <div class="text-[11px] font-mono text-slate-500 pt-2 border-t border-slate-100">
              A weak model can struggle with complex reasoning and tasks that need several steps.
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Term 2: Context -->
          <div class="p-6 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-xs space-y-2">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
              <span class="font-mono font-bold text-lg text-blue-600">C: Context quality</span>
              <span class="text-[10px] font-mono px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 text-blue-700">Second brain</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed font-sans">
              Static context (company principles, voice, guidelines in <code class="font-mono text-slate-800">AGENTS.md</code>, client history in Markdown) and dynamic context (emails, customer records). Automatic updates, stale-note alerts, and contradiction audits help keep it current.
            </p>
            <div class="text-[11px] font-mono text-slate-500 pt-2 border-t border-slate-100">
              Missing or stale context can lead to made-up details, outdated answers, or conflicting business rules.
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Term 3: SOP -->
          <div class="p-6 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-xs space-y-2">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
              <span class="font-mono font-bold text-lg text-amber-700">Skills: Reusable instructions</span>
              <span class="text-[10px] font-mono px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-amber-100 text-amber-800">Procedures</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed font-sans">
              Agent skills package reusable instructions, scripts and resources. Business skills can implement standard operating procedures (SOPs), with clear inputs, approval rules and reviewable outputs.
            </p>
            <div class="text-[11px] font-mono text-slate-500 pt-2 border-t border-slate-100">
              Without shared skills, each employee writes their own prompts and gets inconsistent results.
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Term 4: Tools -->
          <div class="p-6 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-xs space-y-2">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
              <span class="font-mono font-bold text-lg text-emerald-700">Tools: Access to business systems</span>
              <span class="text-[10px] font-mono px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800">Actions</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed font-sans">
              Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers, local file system read/write, terminal scripts, CRM endpoints, and public registry or billing APIs.
            </p>
            <div class="text-[11px] font-mono text-slate-500 pt-2 border-t border-slate-100">
              Without tools, the agent can give advice but cannot update files or business systems.
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>

        <!-- Why Multiplication Matters Alert -->
        <div class="p-6 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/60 border border-amber-200 text-slate-800 text-sm font-sans space-y-2">
          <strong class="text-amber-900 block font-semibold">
            What happens when a factor is missing
          </strong>
          <p class="leading-relaxed">
            Multiplication makes the dependency easy to see: if a factor is zero, the product is zero.
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-1.5 font-mono text-xs text-slate-700 list-disc pl-5">
            <li>A model with no company context may invent details about your business.</li>
            <li>With context but no shared skills, team members have to write their own prompts for each task.</li>
            <li>With context and skills but no tools, the agent can give advice in chat. It cannot write files or update systems.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 pt-2 font-sans">
            YAIBOS describes how to connect these four parts in a system you control.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: The Lock-In Danger -->
      <section class="space-y-6 mb-16 pt-8 border-t border-slate-200/80">
        <div>
          <span class="text-xs font-mono text-red-600 font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider block mb-1">
            Vendor dependence
          </span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 font-sans">
            The cost of depending on one vendor
          </h2>
        </div>

        <p class="text-slate-700 text-base sm:text-lg leading-relaxed font-sans">
          OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google bundle memory, agent tools, execution environments, and interfaces in their platforms.
        </p>

        <p class="text-slate-700 text-base sm:text-lg leading-relaxed font-sans">
          Before putting your business processes in one platform, consider what it would take to leave:
        </p>

        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm font-sans">
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 space-y-1.5">
            <strong class="text-slate-900 block text-base font-semibold">1. Your processes and knowledge</strong>
            <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
              Your lead qualification criteria, pricing formulas, client history, and internal procedures shape how your business works. Keeping them inside an opaque cloud interface makes you depend on that vendor to access and use them.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 space-y-1.5">
            <strong class="text-slate-900 block text-base font-semibold">2. How the provider uses your data</strong>
            <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
              Providers keep training new models. If a platform uses your operational data and solutions for training, those contributions may benefit models your competitors also use.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 space-y-1.5">
            <strong class="text-slate-900 block text-base font-semibold">3. Outages, pricing, and access changes</strong>
            <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
              An outage or a change in terms can interrupt your work. A provider could also raise prices, say by 500%, or restrict access because of international regulations. If your company depends entirely on its harness, you may have no way to keep those operations running.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: Freedom to Switch -->
      <section class="space-y-6 mb-16 pt-8 border-t border-slate-200/80">
        <div>
          <span class="text-xs font-mono text-emerald-700 font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider block mb-1">
            Changing tools
          </span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 font-sans">
            Changing models and harnesses
          </h2>
        </div>

        <p class="text-slate-700 text-base sm:text-lg leading-relaxed font-sans">
          The model does the reasoning. The harness gives it instructions, tools, and an interface. Keeping your data separate lets you change either one.
        </p>

        <p class="text-slate-700 text-base sm:text-lg leading-relaxed font-sans">
          Models and tools change quickly. Your setup should let you choose what fits the work:
        </p>

        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-xs font-mono">
          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white space-y-2">
            <span class="text-blue-600 font-bold block text-sm">Providers change</span>
            <p class="text-slate-600 font-sans text-xs leading-relaxed">
              A provider that suits you today may be overtaken by another. A setup tied to one vendor makes switching harder.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white space-y-2">
            <span class="text-emerald-700 font-bold block text-sm">Match the model to the task</span>
            <p class="text-slate-600 font-sans text-xs leading-relaxed">
              Complex code refactoring calls for tools such as Claude Code or Antigravity. Batch extraction can run faster and 10x cheaper on DeepSeek. Highly sensitive medical or financial data requires a local offline model (Ollama / vLLM) or an LLM provider with strong privacy guarantees, such as Zero Data Retention (ZDR) or private enterprise endpoints.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-blue-50/50 border border-blue-200 text-slate-800 text-sm font-sans space-y-2">
          <strong class="text-blue-900 block font-semibold text-base">
            How YAIBOS keeps skills portable
          </strong>
          <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700">
            Your second brain, skill sources, and tool descriptions live in files you control. The chosen model and connectors determine what data leaves your machine during execution.
          </p>
          <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700">
            To change harness, keep the same source files and run synchronization for a supported destination. Native skill packaging, tool connections, authentication and permissions need verification. Cockpit features may require a different integration.
          </p>
          <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700">
            Schedule intent stays in your second brain. Moving a schedule requires authorization, stopping the previous task and verifying the replacement. Installing skills in another harness does not automatically activate a second schedule.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4: What True Sovereignty Looks Like -->
      <section class="space-y-6 mb-16 pt-8 border-t border-slate-200/80">
        <div>
          <span class="text-xs font-mono text-purple-700 font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider block mb-1">
            Daily work
          </span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 font-sans">
            What you keep control of
          </h2>
        </div>

        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-6 text-sm font-sans">
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 space-y-2">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs text-blue-600 font-bold block">1. File over app</span>
            <strong class="text-slate-900 block text-base font-semibold">Readable files</strong>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
              Following <a href="https://stephango.com/file-over-app" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Steph Ango’s “File over app” principle</a>, YAIBOS keeps business context and procedures in plain Markdown files readable with any text editor.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 space-y-2">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs text-emerald-700 font-bold block">2. Your own AI plan</span>
            <strong class="text-slate-900 block text-base font-semibold">Bring your own subscription</strong>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
              Team members use their own AI plan through the official app or harness (Claude Code with a Claude plan, Codex with a ChatGPT plan). The founder does not get surprise metered API bills.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 space-y-2">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs text-amber-700 font-bold block">3. Reusable procedures</span>
            <strong class="text-slate-900 block text-base font-semibold">Skills and optional launch buttons</strong>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
              Base runs business skills through an existing harness. An optional Cockpit adds launch buttons, progress, approvals and exportable history for its supported harnesses.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Bottom CTAs -->
      <div class="p-8 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-between gap-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 font-sans">
            Read the specification
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-600">
            See the normative requirements, YAML schemas, and safety rules.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 font-mono text-xs shrink-0">
          <a
            href="/spec"
            class="px-5 py-2.5 rounded-lg bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-bold transition-all shadow-xs">
            Read the spec
          </a>
          <a
            href="/about"
            class="px-5 py-2.5 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white hover:bg-slate-100 text-slate-800 font-semibold transition-all shadow-xs">
            About Samuel
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </main>

  <NewsletterSignup variant="card" />
  <MyVideoAskSection />
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
